preConditionHook:
// A GAP IS A MAGNET. The empty band a breakout leaves behind draws price back,
// so its NEAR EDGE is a target for a position taken away from it. A LONG is
// closed at the near edge of the last DOWN gap left ABOVE the market -- the
// high of the bar that gapped below its predecessor -- and a SHORT at the near
// edge of the last UP gap left BELOW it, the low of the bar that gapped above
// its predecessor. The merged pseudocode spelled the up-gap, which is the
// SHORT's cover target; the pair is mirrored, so it is written here with the
// long side first, the same correction ExtremeTestAfterTrendLineBreak needed.
//
// THE LEVEL PERSISTS FROM THE BAR THAT MADE THE GAP, which is the whole
// difference between this and a window scan: a gap ten bars back and a gap
// eighty bars back are the same magnet. GapFilledExit, the nearest catalog
// rule, is the OPPOSITE event on the OPPOSITE side -- it exits a long when the
// close falls BACK BELOW the lower edge of a bull gap, i.e. when the gap fails,
// and it rescans a bounded window every bar rather than holding the level.
// BreakoutLevelRetest's level is a swing pivot, not a gap; IslandReversalRetest
// needs a MATCHED PAIR of gaps with a non-overlap constraint and fires on a
// subset of these bars.
//
// STATEMENT ORDER IS PART OF THE SPECIFICATION, and it is what keeps a gap bar
// from firing on itself: the exit tests are evaluated against the levels held
// COMING INTO the bar, a reached level is then disarmed, and only after that is
// this bar's own gap recorded. A bear-gap bar's high IS its level, so recording
// first would fire the long unconditionally on every down gap.
//
// THE LEVEL IS MARKET STRUCTURE, NOT POSITION STATE, so it survives across
// trades and is never reset on BarsSinceEntry -- the same call
// TrailStopToMicroMeasuringGap makes for its gap level. It is disarmed once
// reached, because a filled gap is no longer a magnet; the arming is a bool
// rather than a level > 0 test, because a back-adjusted continuous contract can
// price below zero. The MarketPosition guard is what makes it an Exit: the
// level is computed every bar whether or not a position is open.
//
// The gap tests are STRICT (Low > High[1]), matching TrueGapBar's measured
// spelling: a bar merely touching the prior bar's extreme leaves no band.
// THE TICK is EL's own MinMove / PriceScale, both VERIFIED and per-symbol
// constants for the whole run, written DIVISION-FIRST in both twins so the C++
// reads token for token against its EasyLanguage (README "N ticks",
// TICKSIZE_FINDINGS.md). Read once into a local; it needs no history.
//
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the generator
// only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration is a redefinition
// when this rule is placed twice in one strategy. Every one is fully assigned
// each bar before it is read.
tickSz = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
exitUp = haveBear && el_ge(high[0], bearLevel - toleranceTicks * tickSz);
exitDn = haveBull && el_le(low[0], bullLevel + toleranceTicks * tickSz);
if (exitUp) haveBear = false;
if (exitDn) haveBull = false;
if (el_gt(low[0], high[1])) {
    bullLevel = low[0];
    haveBull = true;
}
if (el_lt(high[0], low[1])) {
    bearLevel = high[0];
    haveBear = true;
}
